      id: 's15', type: 'explain', skill: 'sn1',
      html: '<p><b>SN1</b> has two steps. First the halide leaves by itself (slow), leaving a flat <b>carbocation</b>; then the nucleophile bonds to it from either face, so a new stereocentre comes out <b>racemic</b>. Only the halide is in the slow step: rate = k[RX], whatever the nucleophile. It needs a stable cation (3° > 2°; a plain 1° never) and a protic solvent, which helps the halide leave.</p>',
      visual: { widget: 'energy-diagram', config: { levels: SN1_3, label: 'via a 3° carbocation', overlay: { levels: SN1_2, label: 'via a 2° carbocation' }, mode: 'view', caption: 'The 3° cation sits lower, so its first hill is lower and SN1 is faster. Illustrative energies, not measured values.' } }
