      id: 's29', type: 'explain', skill: 'sn2',
      html: '<p>Three routes to R–X. <b>HX</b> turns a 3° alcohol into a halide (SN1: water leaves, X⁻ attacks the cation). <b>PBr₃</b> or <b>SOCl₂</b> suit 1° and 2° alcohols: the OH becomes a good leaving group and the halide attacks from the back (SN2, so <b>inversion</b>). An alkane with Cl₂ and light also works, but it is not selective: every kind of C–H is attacked.</p>'
